%% Drone Web (fictional shop): build vs buy, after Will Larson's use of Wardley maps in
%% Crafting Engineering Strategy (lethain.com/wardley-mapping). Strategy marks:
%% (build) = build in house (Core), (buy) = buy a product, (outsource) = rent a service.
%% Compatibility rules depend on product specs. Rule changes are also approved
%% (SubmitRuleChangeForApproval in boundary-map.md); that link is left out to keep
%% the map readable: the build/buy point does not depend on it.
%% Payment is rented as a service: the provider runs it, so the map stops there.
%% leaf "Payment"
%% Product Lifecycle Management, the one source of truth for product data, is Supporting with a
%% Core spec model (D-037 in .ralph/agent/decisions.md). So it shows as two components:
%% Spec model: the specs the compatibility rules read (voltage, current, mounting, protocol).
%% It is the shop's own, so it is built (P1, P7). Its stream, product-specifications, is the
%% Core contract.
%% Product data hub: product records, lifecycle states and replacement links. Every shop has
%% them, so buy a product information system behind an ACL, or build the hub thin (P1).
%% The spec model needs the hub: specs describe a product that the hub identifies.
%% Inventory: built thin (P1), so it has no build or buy mark. Stock counts come from the ERP
%% (P1, diagnosis point 7). The ERP is the system of record the business already uses; it is
%% bought, not designed here, so the map stops there.
%% leaf "ERP"
%% Approval workflow: Catalog Governance Workflow, the one boundary typed Generic.
note "Build left of centre, buy or rent right of it" [0.04, 0.04]
